--TEST--
`$name as class<T>` throws where the conversion is written when the name denotes no class that is a `T` (`rule:types/class-reference`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `as` is a class reference's only source, so it is also the only place the
// answer can be wrong — every site downstream of it holds a descriptor that
// already passed. That is what makes this a checked conversion rather than a
// cast: the failure is reported where the string was, not where the class
// reference was later used.
class Formatter {
    public function render(string $body): string {
        return $body;
    }
}

class Angled extends Formatter {
}

// A class, and not one of the classes this unit declares to be a `Formatter`.
class Ledger {
}

string $missing = "Nothing";
try {
    class<Formatter> $bad = $missing as class<Formatter>;
    echo "unreachable: ", (new $bad())->render("x"), "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}

string $unrelated = "Ledger";
try {
    class<Formatter> $sibling = $unrelated as class<Formatter>;
    echo "unreachable: ", (new $sibling())->render("x"), "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $wrong) {
    echo $wrong->message, "\n";
}

// Narrowing a reference names the class the reference holds.
class<Formatter> $wide = "Formatter" as class<Formatter>;
try {
    class<Angled> $narrow = $wide as class<Angled>;
    echo "unreachable: ", (new $narrow())->render("x"), "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $narrowed) {
    echo $narrowed->message, "\n";
}

// The same conversion over a name that is in the hierarchy answers instead —
// the throw is about this program's own class graph and nothing else.
string $good = "Angled";
class<Formatter> $ok = $good as class<Formatter>;
echo (new $ok())->render("done"), "\n";
--EXPECT--
cannot convert `Nothing` to `class<Formatter>`: it does not name a class that is a `Formatter`
cannot convert `Ledger` to `class<Formatter>`: it does not name a class that is a `Formatter`
cannot convert `Formatter` to `class<Angled>`: it does not name a class that is a `Angled`
done
